{
  "dom_id": "step-e14820",
  "name": "Example: Finding an Unknown Parameter",
  "problem": "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation $(x+3)^2+10=k?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+10 & =k \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =k-10 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k-10} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\sqrt{k-10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{k-10} & =0 \\\\ k-10 & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ will give only one solution to the equation.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
